<title>USA: U.S. July import prices fell 0.2 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. July import prices fell 0.2 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-08-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Labor Department report of U.S. import and export price changes by percent (unadjusted).</p>
<p>    -			   July   June-Rev from  July97/96</p>
<p> ALL IMPORTS		  -0.2   Unch    0.4     -1.9</p>
<p> -Petroleum		   -0.7   -1.8    2.4     -8.2</p>
<p> -Nonpetroleum		-0.1   Unch    0.2     -1.5</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	 -1.9    0.2    1.1	2.7</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies	0.1   -0.6    0.9     -2.0</p>
<p> -Capital Goods	     -0.4    0.1    0.2     -5.6</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts     0.2    0.5    0.5	0.5</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos  Unch   -0.1   -0.1     -0.5</p>
<p>Percent changes, not seasonally adjusted --</p>
<p> ALL EXPORTS		  -0.1   Unch   -0.1     -2.3</p>
<p> -Agricultural		-0.9   -1.9   -1.9    -19.0</p>
<p> -Non-Agricultural	  Unch    0.2    0.1	0.1</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	 -0.9   -1.9   -1.9    -21.2</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies     Unch    0.3    0.4	0.7</p>
<p> -Capital Goods	     -0.1   Unch    0.1     -0.7</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts     0.2   Unch   Unch	1.3</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos   0.2    0.1   Unch	0.9</p>
<p>The department reported July price indexes (1995 base year equals 100) of 98.1 for imports and 98.6 for exports.  </p>
<p>Both petroleum and nonpetroleum import prices contributed to the decline in overall July import prices, the Labor department said. Imported petroleum prices fell 23.3 percent over the past six months.</p>
<p>The dip in export prices was led by a fall in agricultural export prices, primarily because of continued declines in grain prices, the department said. Prices for agricultural exports fell 10.6 percent over the past four months and 19 percent for the 12 months ended in July.</p>
<p>((Washington newsroom, 202 898-8310))</p>
